          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %strong tileorder
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %em double order
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            equipped with two
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+orders"} partial orders
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \le
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊑
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sqsubseteq
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ⊑
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\sqsubseteq b
            iff
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            “lies below”
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\le b
            iff
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            “lies to the left of”
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi b
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
          %p
            Such orders are of interest in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+category"} double category
            theory, since these are the arrangements of 2-cells in a double category which could potentially be composed (although in a general double category, not all of them can actually be composed).
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            Suppose given a dissection of a rectangle into finitely many subrectangles. Define
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            to be the set of subrectangles, and for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a,b\in T
            write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ≤
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\le b
            if there exists a finite list
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi …
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi n
                  %mo =
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a= x_0, x_1, \dots, x_n = b
            such that for each
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_i
            intersects the left edge of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_{i+1}
            in more than one point. Define
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ⊑
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\sqsubseteq b
            similarly using top and bottom edges instead of left and right. Clearly
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \le
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊑
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sqsubseteq
            are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+orders"} partial orders
          %p
            A set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            equipped with two partial orders
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ≤
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \le
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊑
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sqsubseteq
            (a priori, unrelated) is called a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong double order
            = succeed "." do
              %strong tileorder
          .query
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Todd+Trimble"}> Todd
              \: Maybe I’m missing what you mean by “dissection”, but off the bat it looks like you are allowing the “pinwheel” as a possible dissection (I hope it is clear what the pinwheel is; Dawson discusses it in one of his papers), but this is the kind of configuration not interpretable in double categories.
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"}> Mike Shulman
              \: Yes, we are allowing the pinwheel, even though it is not always composable in a double category. That’s what I meant to imply above by
            %blockquote
              %p arrangements of 2-cells in a double category which could potentially be composed (although in a general double category, not all of them can actually be composed)
            %p
              but maybe that isn’t sufficiently clear. The notion of tileorder is purely geometric/combinatorial, and you then have to ask which tileorders are composable in a double category (essentially, all that don’t contain a pinwheel). (BTW, a picture of the pinwheel can be found
              = succeed ".)" do
                %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/double+category#unbiased_composition_and_associativity_9"} here
          %h2#characterizations Characterizations
          %p As proven by Dawson and Paré, tileorders can be characterized in purely combinatorial terms in a couple of interesting ways.
          %h3#using_maximal_chain_properties Using maximal chain properties
          %p
            A double order
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            is said to have the
            %strong
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊏
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sqsubset
              \-parallel maximal chain property
            if whenever
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            are maximal
            = succeed "-chains" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊑
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sqsubseteq
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
